  "body": "## Purpose\n\nStop DeepSeek-V4.1 decode CUDA graphs from pinning a private copy of the sparse-attention page metadata, which made `ds41-flash` (DSpark) servers at `--gpu-memory-utilization 0.95` intermittently run out of memory shortly after startup.\n\n## Root cause\n\nd11d4f35a8 computes decode page metadata once per KV-cache group per forward, in `torch.empty` tensors outside the workspace arena. Those per-forward tensors were registered as CUDA graph capture resources, both directly and through the scratch list that also holds them, so every captured graph kept its own copy. At the full 1M context (page-table width 4096, decode capacity 480 rows for 32 seqs \u00d7 (1 + 2\u00b77) DSpark rows) that is 116.6 MiB per graph: 3.8 GiB per GPU over the 33 FULL verification graphs.\n\nAn allocation-history probe during capture attributed 100% of the retained graph-pool memory per target graph (116.55 MiB, 66 blocks) to this one allocation site.\n\nThe graph memory profiler samples the two largest FULL graphs and extrapolates the rest by token count, so it cannot see a constant per-graph cost. It reserved 1.8 GiB for graphs that took 5.3 GiB. The KV cache was sized ~3.5 GiB too large and only ~1.2 GiB stayed free after startup. Lazily loaded kernels (the sampler top-p Triton kernel, adaptive-verification Inductor regions) then hit `CUDA error: out of memory` in 2 of 10 DSpark server runs on the stock beta image.\n\nThis follows the ownership rule of #780 (retain borrowed scratch, not activations): d11d4f35a8 moved the metadata out of the arena, and the existing retention then pinned it.\n\n## Change\n\n- Every replay rewrites the metadata before reading it, so it is an ordinary graph-pool activation: retain only the borrowed workspace arena. The metadata is still computed once per KV-cache group per forward; the graph pool now shares its storage across graph shapes.\n- `test_attention_shared_scratch_graph_replay` now gives every forward a fresh context (as `set_forward_context` does). After capture it poisons the captured decode metadata and asserts that it is released and that replays stay bit-exact.\n\nNo estimator, profile or b12x change.\n\n## Measurements\n\nds41-flash profile, DSpark K7, TP4 on 4\u00d7 RTX PRO 6000 (SM120), GMU 0.95, 1M context, RAM Engram. Same image; only the model sources are overlaid (current beta vLLM `c7b5e86bd2` + b12x #435).\n\n| Per GPU | Before (beta) | After |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| CUDA graph memory, actual | 5.34 GiB | **1.90 GiB** |\n| CUDA graph memory, estimated | 1.82 GiB | 1.83 GiB |\n| KV cache | 9.94 GiB / 9,638,983 tokens | 9.93 GiB / 9,650,320 tokens |\n| Free after startup | ~1.2 GiB | **~4.1 GiB** |\n\nDSpark serving workload (mixed long generations, 2 repetitions; C1/C8/C32 tok/s, accepted tokens per draft):\n\n| | C1 | C8 | C32 | Acceptance |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Before (beta) | 231\u2013239 | 666\u2013667 | 1338\u20131362 | ~1.8 |\n| After | 228\u2013234 | 671\u2013680 | 1333\u20131342 | 1.74\u20131.85 |\n\nThree independent server starts with the fix each passed the workload with zero errors. `nvidia-smi` stayed at 93.6\u201393.7 of 97.9 GB used before and after the workload, and the estimate was 1.81\u20131.83 GiB against 1.90\u20131.92 GiB actual.\n\nWith exact per-graph accounting (`VLLM_DEBUG_GRAPH_MEMORY_ACCOUNTING=1`), every target FULL graph now adds +0.0 MiB of allocated pool memory (previously +116.6 MiB). The pool grows only for the first graph (+270 MiB) and once at 96 tokens (+78 MiB).\n\nIn that debug mode the profiler now over-estimates (5.14 GiB estimated vs 1.75 GiB actual). `torch.cuda.graph()` calls `empty_cache()` on entry, after the profiler has read free memory, so each per-graph sample subtracts the eager warmup's released cache (here, the warmup's own metadata). The same bias hid this regression from the default estimator: the retained 116.6 MiB per graph was cancelled out by the warmup's released 116 MiB. The default (sampling) estimate is barely affected; the estimator is left for a separate change.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `tests/v1/attention/test_b12x_v41_workspace.py::test_attention_shared_scratch_graph_replay` on RTX PRO 6000 (SM120): 18 passed with this PR. With the test change but without the ownership fix, the 9 decode cases fail with `Capture resources retained caller activations: ['decode_metadata', ...]`.\n- Whole `test_b12x_v41_workspace.py`: 43 passed, 7 failed. The same 7 fail without this PR:\n  - 4 output-projection tests: the test layer helper lacks `_l2pf_ffn` since d11d4f35a8.\n  - 3 CED compact-attention tests: their FP8 arithmetic oracle predates the BF16 attention default of #911; follow-up.\n- `pre-commit run --files` on the changed files: ruff, ruff-format and repository hooks pass. mypy reports the same 9 errors in `attention.py` as the base commit.\n- Release fragment validated with `release_changelog.validate_fragment`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Not a duplicate: open PRs #780 and #511 target `dev/jovian-judgement` and predate d11d4f35a8. No open beta PR touches DS4.1 decode metadata ownership.\n- AI assistance (Claude Code) was used to investigate, implement and validate this change.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
